[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The CS gateway/port (router0 eth1, 192.168.2.1) and its uplink to the CS switch (switch1 eth0/eth1) are healthy and not flapping. Evidence: on router0, ethtool for eth1 reports Link detected: yes and ip -s link shows zero errors/drops. The physical carrier counters confirm stability: /sys/class/net/eth1 shows carrier_changes=2 with 1 up and 1 down (initial bring-up only). On switch1, both eth0 (to router0) and eth1 (to lan_cs) show the same single up/down since boot and Link detected: yes, with no queue drops. The bridge is in forwarding state on both ports and its FDB contains the expected CS host MACs. Targeted tests from CS hosts show normal connectivity: cs_lab1 and cs_lab3 can continuously reach 192.168.2.1 with 0% loss and sub‑ms RTT; cs_lab1 reaches the “internet” LAN (pc2, 128.168.0.2) with 0% loss. Topology-wide reachability is also normal.

Mechanism behind perceived “blinking”: the switch/router port LEDs blink with traffic, not link up/down. Brief pauses users feel can occur when ARP neighbor entries age to STALE/DELAY and are refreshed; we observed normal neighbor state transitions (DELAY/REACHABLE) on both a CS host and router0, which can add a momentary first‑packet delay, but this is not a port failure.

Root cause: No network/port fault; observed LED activity was misinterpreted as link flapping.

Corrective fix: None required. Recommend monitoring for DNS/application issues, and optionally tune ARP timers if first‑packet delays are user-noticeable. Ports should not be restarted. 
=== END ===